# Copy to .env and edit. Only JWT_SECRET_KEY has no working default -- everything else
# here shows the value the code uses when the variable is absent, so you can see what a
# setting is before deciding to change it.

# =========================================================================================
# WEB LAYER
# =========================================================================================

# Required. Signs the login tokens. No default on purpose: a fallback secret works in
# development, ships to production unnoticed, and makes every token it ever signed
# forgeable by anyone who has read the source. Generate one with:
#     python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))"
JWT_SECRET_KEY=

# How long a login lasts.
JWT_EXPIRY_HOURS=24

# Where the browser is. Comma-separate to allow more than one origin.
FRONTEND_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# Server-side password rule. The "at least one number" rule is not configurable.
# PASSWORD_MIN_LENGTH=8

# --- Start-up warming --------------------------------------------------------------------
# What this process pays for at boot instead of making the first user pay for it. Two
# separate switches, because they are very different sizes of bill.

# The 90 MB embedding model and the ingestion import chain: ~16s, and every upload needs
# them. On by default. Worth turning off only if you are restarting constantly and not
# uploading anything.
# API_WARM_UP=1

# The two ~2 GB GGUFs. Off by default, and that default is aimed at development: four
# gigabytes read at every restart makes --reload unusable. Turn it ON for a demo, where the
# same cost otherwise lands inside the first question somebody asks -- both models loaded,
# plus the Metal shader compilation that happens on a model's first generation rather than
# when it is constructed. Start-up gets slower by roughly what the first chat turn gets
# faster; the difference is that nobody is watching the first one.
# API_WARM_MODELS=1

# =========================================================================================
# DATABASES        docker compose up -d
# =========================================================================================

# MongoDB holds everything that is not a vector: the PDFs (GridFS), the cleaned page text,
# accounts, session bindings, chat history, generated resources, the evaluation log and the
# job queue. Losing this loses the corpus; losing Qdrant only costs a re-ingest.
#
# 27018 rather than 27017, because 27017 is often already answered by a native MongoDB
# service shared with other projects, and sharing a server means sharing a failure --
# another project's `docker compose down -v` would take this one's data with it.
LEARNMATE_MONGODB_URI=mongodb://localhost:27018
LEARNMATE_MONGODB_DB=learnmate

# Where the chunk embeddings live.
#   qdrant   a Qdrant server: a real HNSW index, filtering and scoring server-side. Default.
#   mongodb  keep them in MongoDB instead, so there is no second service to run.
# Switching does not migrate anything -- re-ingest to populate the new one.
LEARNMATE_VECTOR_BACKEND=qdrant

# A URL, never a directory: a path would run Qdrant inside this process and lock the
# directory, so only one process could hold the corpus. 6335 rather than 6333 for the same
# reason as the Mongo port above; docker-compose.yml publishes the matching port.
LEARNMATE_QDRANT_URL=http://localhost:6335
LEARNMATE_QDRANT_API_KEY=
LEARNMATE_QDRANT_COLLECTION=learnmate_chunks
# LEARNMATE_QDRANT_TIMEOUT=30
# LEARNMATE_QDRANT_BATCH_SIZE=128

# Only used when LEARNMATE_VECTOR_BACKEND=mongodb: the name of the Atlas vector index.
# Ignored off Atlas, where scoring falls back to exact NumPy cosine.
LEARNMATE_VECTOR_INDEX=chunk_vector_index

# =========================================================================================
# MODELS
# =========================================================================================
# Three backends, per role: "llamacpp" (a local GGUF, default), "http" (an
# OpenAI-compatible endpoint) or "gemini" (the Google API).
#
# Keep the generator and the judge on different model families whichever you pick. A judge
# sharing the generator's weights rates its own output style highly and the quality gate
# stops firing -- which looks like everything passing rather than like a broken evaluator.

# --- Generator: writes chat replies and study resources ----------------------------------
LEARNMATE_GENERATOR_BACKEND=llamacpp
LEARNMATE_GENERATOR_MODEL=models/qwen2.5-3b-instruct-q4_k_m.gguf
LEARNMATE_GENERATOR_N_CTX=4096
#
# Or the domain finetune from the ML track (model-Thevindu): the LoRA adapter
# `qwen25-lora-20260815-090709` -- QLoRA on Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct over the lm-legal-v0.1
# Sri Lankan legal corpus -- merged into its base and converted to GGUF. It ships as an
# adapter, not a GGUF, so build it first (~10 min, one off, needs `peft` and `gguf`):
#
#     python scripts/build_finetuned_gguf.py
#
# Both REPO and FILE must be emptied with it. They are the download source for a missing
# GGUF, and this one is built locally and exists in no repository -- left at the base
# model's values, a missing build silently downloads the stock 3B and runs that under the
# finetune's name.
#
# Worth knowing before switching: it is 1.5B against the base's 3B, so it is a weaker
# general model, and it did not pass the ML track's acceptance gate -- 0.557 / 0.621
# LLM-judge accuracy against a 0.70 minimum, losing to the API fallback on accuracy and
# groundedness (model-Thevindu/03_testing_and_versioning/version_registry.csv). Better on
# statutory Q&A, weaker elsewhere, and not a promotion.
#
# LEARNMATE_GENERATOR_MODEL=models/learnmate-legal-qwen2.5-1.5b-q8_0.gguf
# LEARNMATE_GENERATOR_REPO=
# LEARNMATE_GENERATOR_FILE=
#
# Served over an OpenAI-compatible HTTP API instead:
# LEARNMATE_GENERATOR_BACKEND=http
# LEARNMATE_GENERATOR_API_URL=http://localhost:8001/v1
# LEARNMATE_GENERATOR_MODEL=learnmate-finetuned
# LEARNMATE_GENERATOR_API_KEY=
#
# Or through Gemini -- seconds instead of ~30, at the cost of sending the text off the
# machine. Set GEMINI_API_KEY below.
# LEARNMATE_GENERATOR_BACKEND=gemini
# LEARNMATE_GENERATOR_MODEL=gemini-2.0-flash

# Only needed if a finetune uses a prompt template llama.cpp cannot read from the GGUF
# metadata. Leave empty for anything finetuned from a standard base.
LEARNMATE_GENERATOR_CHAT_FORMAT=
# Where a missing GGUF is downloaded from, on first run only. Ignored once the file exists,
# so a hand-placed finetune is never overwritten.
# LEARNMATE_GENERATOR_REPO=Qwen/Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct-GGUF
# LEARNMATE_GENERATOR_FILE=qwen2.5-3b-instruct-q4_k_m.gguf

# --- Judge: grades what the generator wrote ----------------------------------------------
LEARNMATE_JUDGE_BACKEND=llamacpp
LEARNMATE_JUDGE_MODEL=models/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct-Q4_K_M.gguf
# Larger than the generator's on purpose: the judge reads a resource *and* the passage it
# must be faithful to, so its input is the longer of the two.
LEARNMATE_JUDGE_N_CTX=8192
# LEARNMATE_JUDGE_BACKEND=gemini
# LEARNMATE_JUDGE_MODEL=gemini-2.0-flash-lite
# LEARNMATE_JUDGE_REPO=bartowski/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct-GGUF
# LEARNMATE_JUDGE_FILE=Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct-Q4_K_M.gguf
# LEARNMATE_JUDGE_CHAT_FORMAT=

# Read only when a role's backend is "gemini".
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Where the GGUF files live, and where a missing one is downloaded to.
# LEARNMATE_MODELS_DIR=models

# --- How the models are executed ---------------------------------------------------------
# The largest single lever on reply latency, and the one block in this file that is NOT
# portable: these settings describe the machine, not the model. The values below are the
# code defaults; the two profiles underneath are what they should become on a real machine.
# Measure before copying either one.

# Decode threads. 0 lets llama.cpp pick, which is half the logical processors -- a fair
# guess on a uniform CPU and a poor one on a hybrid, where counting Intel E-cores or Apple
# efficiency cores as equals spreads work onto cores that finish late.
LEARNMATE_N_THREADS=0
# Prefill threads, a separate knob because reading the prompt scales with cores while
# writing tokens is memory-bound and stops scaling early. 0 leaves llama.cpp's default of
# every logical processor.
LEARNMATE_N_THREADS_BATCH=0

# Layers on the GPU. 0 is CPU only; -1 offloads the whole model, which is what a Metal Mac
# wants -- a 3B Q4 model is ~2 GB and fits in unified memory entire. Values in between
# split the model and pay a transfer per token, so 0 and -1 are the settings that matter.
# On Windows this needs a CUDA or Vulkan build of llama-cpp-python; the CPU wheel ignores it.
LEARNMATE_N_GPU_LAYERS=0

# Prefill batch size: tokens processed per pass. Bigger means fewer, larger matrix
# multiplies, which suits a GPU; on CPU the gain flattens and scratch memory grows.
LEARNMATE_N_BATCH=512

# Flash attention. Note this is not a no-op when off: llama-cpp-python's boolean reaches
# only llama.cpp's ENABLED or DISABLED, never its AUTO, so leaving it unset forces the slow
# attention path even on a backend that supports the fast one. Off here only to preserve
# existing behaviour -- turn it on anywhere you have measured it, and always with a GPU.
LEARNMATE_FLASH_ATTN=0

# Pin the weights in RAM so they cannot be paged out. Good on a machine with memory to
# spare; bad on one already running two databases and a browser, where it pushes those into
# swap instead. Needs privileges, and llama.cpp warns and continues when it lacks them.
LEARNMATE_USE_MLOCK=0

# --- Profile: a CPU-only laptop ------------------------------------------------------------
# Expect single-digit tokens per second and accept it; on a memory-bandwidth-bound part the
# thread count barely moves the number. Measured on an Intel Core 7 150U: every setting from
# 4 to 10 threads landed within 2.9-3.2 tok/s.
# LEARNMATE_N_THREADS=8
# LEARNMATE_N_THREADS_BATCH=8
# LEARNMATE_N_GPU_LAYERS=0
# LEARNMATE_N_BATCH=1024
# LEARNMATE_FLASH_ATTN=1
# LEARNMATE_USE_MLOCK=0

# --- Profile: an Apple Silicon Mac (Metal) -------------------------------------------------
# The one line that matters is N_GPU_LAYERS=-1. Everything else is a refinement on top of it.
# LEARNMATE_N_GPU_LAYERS=-1
# Performance cores only -- `sysctl -n hw.perflevel0.physicalcpu`.
# LEARNMATE_N_THREADS=<P-CORES>
# LEARNMATE_N_THREADS_BATCH=<P-CORES>
# LEARNMATE_N_BATCH=512
# LEARNMATE_FLASH_ATTN=1
# LEARNMATE_USE_MLOCK=1

# =========================================================================================
# UPLOADS
# =========================================================================================
# Two limits on the same file, because size alone does not bound the work: a 3 MB scan of
# 800 pages is a small file and a very long ingest.
LEARNMATE_MAX_PDF_MB=10
LEARNMATE_MAX_PAGE_COUNT=300

# A session is about exactly one PDF: a second, different PDF ingested into the same
# session is refused, because embedding is the expensive step here. Set to 0 to allow a
# session to hold several.
LEARNMATE_ONE_PDF_PER_SESSION=1

# =========================================================================================
# RETRIEVAL AND GENERATION
# =========================================================================================
LEARNMATE_EMBEDDING_MODEL=all-MiniLM-L6-v2

# Some retrieval models want an instruction on the query side and lose accuracy without
# it. MiniLM does not, so these are empty by default. To move to bge-small-en-v1.5 --
# same 384 dims and speed, but a 512-token window instead of MiniLM's 256 -- set:
#   LEARNMATE_EMBEDDING_MODEL=BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5
#   LEARNMATE_EMBEDDING_QUERY_PREFIX=Represent this sentence for searching relevant passages:
# and then RE-INGEST every document with force=True. Vectors from two different embedding
# models are not comparable, and the failure is silent: retrieval returns confident,
# meaningless scores. The server warns at start-up if it finds documents in that state.
LEARNMATE_EMBEDDING_QUERY_PREFIX=
LEARNMATE_EMBEDDING_DOC_PREFIX=

# A chunk longer than the embedding model's window is silently truncated and its tail never
# reaches the vector. MiniLM stops at 256 word pieces, so this is a bet on how much text
# 900 characters tokenises to.
#
# The bet holds, and it was checked rather than assumed: tokenising all 755 chunks of this
# project's corpus, nothing was truncated -- median 133 tokens, longest 235 against the 256
# limit. The splitter breaks at sentence boundaries well before the character ceiling.
#
# Re-check rather than trust it if the corpus changes character: 235/256 is 92% of the
# budget, so a document with denser tokenisation (tables, code, numerals) has little room.
# Lowering this to ~600 buys the margin back, at the cost of re-ingesting everything.
LEARNMATE_CHUNK_SIZE=900
LEARNMATE_CHUNK_OVERLAP=150

# How many chunks reach the prompt. With reranking on these are the best of
# LEARNMATE_RERANK_CANDIDATES rather than the vector search's own top few, so fewer chunks
# carry more signal -- and each one dropped is prompt the generator *and* the judge no
# longer have to read.
LEARNMATE_TOP_K=3
# LEARNMATE_MIN_CHUNK_CHARS=80

# --- Reranking ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A cross-encoder re-scores what the vector search found, reading each chunk together with
# the question instead of comparing two independently-made vectors. ~100ms for 20
# candidates against a generation measured in tens of seconds, and it pays for itself
# twice: better context also means fewer replies the judge rejects, and every rejection
# costs a full regeneration.
#
# Needs no new package (sentence-transformers is already required) but does download ~90 MB
# on first use. Set to 0 to fall back to plain vector-search order.
LEARNMATE_RERANK_ENABLED=1
# LEARNMATE_RERANK_MODEL=cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2
# LEARNMATE_RERANK_CANDIDATES=20

# Rerank scores are logits put through a sigmoid, so this sits in [0, 1] like the cosine
# threshold below and 0.5 is the model's own decision boundary. When reranking is on, THIS
# is what decides "pdf" vs "general" mode on a reply.
LEARNMATE_RERANK_THRESHOLD=0.5

# Cosine similarity below which the chat agent stops trusting the PDF and answers from
# general knowledge instead. Only consulted when reranking is off or unavailable.
#
# Model-specific, and not portable across a model change: raw cosine under MiniLM puts even
# unrelated text at 0.1-0.3, while bge-* compresses everything much higher. Retune against
# your own score distribution rather than carrying this number over.
LEARNMATE_RELEVANCE_THRESHOLD=0.25

# Judge scores below this trigger exactly one regeneration. Raising MAX_ATTEMPTS is not
# just slower: a 3B judge tends to oscillate rather than converge over more rounds.
LEARNMATE_EVALUATOR_THRESHOLD=70

# Retrieval modes whose replies skip the LLM judge. Comma-separated; empty judges every
# turn, which is what this did before the gate existed.
#
# The judge is the most expensive step in a turn -- a second 3B model, median 36s on the
# local backend. This measures where that buys something. Across the 21 chat verdicts
# logged by this project: pdf mode scored across the full 1-100 range and rejected about
# two thirds of replies, while general mode returned exactly 100 every single time. With no
# retrieved text there is nothing for the judge to check a reply against, and a score with
# no variance cannot gate anything -- so general mode is skipped and pdf mode, where the
# judge actually catches things, is not.
#
# Skips are logged with stage="gate", so widening this or reverting it stays an
# evidence-based decision rather than a guess.
LEARNMATE_JUDGE_GATE_MODES=general
LEARNMATE_MAX_ATTEMPTS=2
LEARNMATE_MAX_HISTORY_TURNS=6
LEARNMATE_MAX_SOURCE_CHARS=6000

# Lifts anonymous rate limits when a model has to be downloaded from Hugging Face.
HF_TOKEN=

# --- Keycloak ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On, and it has to be: the login page is a redirect to Keycloak now, not a form, so the
# only token a browser can obtain is one Keycloak issued. Turning this off leaves the
# frontend able to log in and the backend refusing every request it then makes.
#
# It does not disable the original path -- deps.py still tries the HS256 token first, so
# anything already holding one from /api/auth/login keeps working. This only *adds* a
# second accepted kind.
#
# (config.py still defaults it to false when the variable is absent entirely, which keeps
# `import app.config` honest for tooling and tests that have no .env at all.)
#
# `docker compose up -d` from this directory brings Keycloak up alongside Mongo and
# Qdrant, publishing it on 8081 (admin console: admin/admin). It seeds the `learnmate`
# realm, a public `learnmate-frontend` client and a dev/dev user from
# keycloak/realm-learnmate.json, so there is nothing to click through by hand.
KEYCLOAK_ENABLED=true
KEYCLOAK_ISSUER=http://localhost:8081/realms/learnmate

# Derived from KEYCLOAK_ISSUER when left unset; set it only if the realm's JWKS lives
# somewhere the standard path does not reach.
KEYCLOAK_JWKS_URL=
